+ * one of them on every `npm test` (code guidelines §4: a fixture that survives
+ * between runs is a fixture a test can silently mutate).
+ *
+ * It could not. Removing a directory and recreating it under the same name
+ * leaves a name that resolves, that `existsSync` and `statSync` both confirm,
+ * and that a freshly spawned git nevertheless cannot use as a working
+ * directory — see `prepareCaseDirectory`. `node test/fixtures/build.mjs`
+ * failed 2 runs in 5 before the fix and 0 in 25 after it.
+ *
+ * This exercises the real path — `buildCase`, not a helper — enough times that
+ * the previous implementation could not have survived it. On a filesystem
+ * without the quirk it passes without proving anything, which is the honest
+ * description of a test for an environment failure.
+ */
+import { strict as assert } from "node:assert";
+import { test } from "node:test";
+import { execFileSync } from "node:child_process";
+import { rmSync } from "node:fs";
